# One checkout, one set of bytes.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout rewrites every
# text file's line endings in the WORKING TREE, and the repository's own tests
# read those working-tree files. One assertion in tests/build.test.mjs is
# anchored to the end of a line — `rewrite … break;$` — so on Windows the
# carriage return sat between the semicolon and the `$` and the test failed on a
# file whose committed content is correct. A test suite that fails only on the
# operating system it was written on trains people to ignore it.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes the repository's canonical form LF everywhere: files
# are normalised on commit, and checked out as LF on every platform. The rules
# below pin the exceptions by hand, because `auto` cannot know what it is
# looking at.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The Windows image of the PHP built-in server is launched from a batch file,
# and a CRLF is what batch reads.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Everything below is binary and must never be touched by a newline conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.sqlite binary
